<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henri van der Noot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1731">1731</a>–<a href="/years/1827">1827</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henri van der Noot was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> lawyer who led the conservative party in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brabant-revolution">Brabant Revolution</a> of 1789 against the reforms of Emperor Joseph II in the Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. From exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> he organised the émigré army that invaded the country in October 1789. His Statists, defending the old privileges of the estates and the Church, dominated the United Belgian States and drove out the democratic Vonckists, but Austrian rule was restored in 1790.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henri-van-der-noot.jpg" alt="Henri van der Noot" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles-Amédée-Philippe van Loo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Henri_van_der_Noot_-_Brussels_City_Museum_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
